name: binary data
intro: A file is bytes. Text is the case where each byte happens to be a
intro: letter; everything else -- images, archives, network packets -- is
intro: bytes that mean numbers. So read and write them as bytes: `fopen`
intro: with "rb", `fread` and `fwrite`, no `fgets`, no `strlen` (a zero
intro: byte is data, not an end).
intro:
intro: A number wider than one byte has to be laid out in some order. The
intro: number 0x01020304 as four bytes is either
intro:
intro:     04 03 02 01    little-endian: least significant first (x86, ARM)
intro:     01 02 03 04    big-endian: most significant first (network order)
intro:
intro: A file format picks one and says so. Your CPU's order must not leak
intro: in. So never do this:
intro:
intro:     uint32_t v = *(uint32_t *)buf;     /* wrong */
intro:
intro: It reads in the machine's order, and `buf` may not be aligned for a
intro: 4-byte load; an unaligned or wrongly-typed access is undefined
intro: behaviour (the type rules are called strict aliasing). Build the
intro: number from bytes instead, which is the same on every machine.
intro:
intro: The idea: each byte is worth 256 times the byte before it (a byte
intro: holds 0 to 255, then you carry). Shifting left by 8 bits multiplies
intro: by 256, so "shift the byte up to its place, then OR it in" builds
intro: the number. Take two bytes, 34 12, read little-endian (first byte
intro: is the low one):
intro:
intro:     b[0] = 0x34 stays where it is           -> 0x0034
intro:     b[1] = 0x12 moves up one byte (<< 8)    -> 0x1200
intro:     OR them together                        -> 0x1234  (4660)
intro:
intro: In C that is one line for 16 bits:
intro:
intro:     uint16_t le = (uint16_t)(b[1] << 8 | b[0]);
intro:
intro: Big-endian is the same trick with the roles swapped: the FIRST
intro: byte is the high one. For a wider number there are more bytes, and
intro: each step up the number is another 8 bits of shift. Work out which
intro: byte gets which shift by asking "how many bytes above the lowest
intro: is it?".
intro:
intro: Cast to the wide type before shifting when the result is wide:
intro: `b[3] << 24` would shift an int, and a byte of 0x80 or more ends up
intro: in the sign bit, which is undefined. Use `unsigned char`, not
intro: `char`, which may be signed.
intro:
intro: A real format has a header: a few fixed "magic" bytes that say what
intro: the file is (0x7f then "ELF" for ELF), a version, a count, then
intro: records of a fixed size, and often a checksum so damage is
intro: noticed. Read the header first, check every field, and never trust a
intro: count until you know the file is long enough to hold it.
intro:
intro: In the cases, `printf '\001\002'` feeds raw bytes: a backslash and
intro: three octal digits is one byte.
---
q: u32dump.c -- read 32-bit numbers from bytes
spec: Read standard input as a sequence of 4-byte unsigned numbers and print
spec: each in decimal on its own line. The order is little-endian by default;
spec: with the argument `be` it is big-endian, with `le` little-endian.
spec:
spec: If the input ends with 1 to 3 bytes that do not make a whole number,
spec: print `u32dump: N stray bytes at the end` to standard error and exit 1. Any other argument, or more than one, prints
spec: `usage: u32dump [be]` and exits 2. Empty input prints nothing.
file: u32dump.c
need: - fread(buf, 1, size, stdin) returns bytes read; short = end
need: - read into unsigned char, never char (may be signed)
need: - build a number: shift each byte up to its place, OR together
need: - little-endian: first byte is the LOW one; big-endian: HIGH
need: - widen first: (uint32_t)b[3] << 24, else the shift is on an int
need: - print a uint32_t as (unsigned long) with %lu
need: - errors go to stderr (fprintf); usage exit 2, stray bytes exit 1
start: #include <stdint.h>
start: #include <stdio.h>
start: #include <string.h>
start: 
start: int main(int argc, char **argv)
start: {
start: 	/* check argv: only none, "be" or "le" is allowed, else usage + 2 */
start: 	(void)argc;
start: 	(void)argv;
start: 	/* read 4 bytes at a time, print each as a number */
start: 	return 0;
start: }
prog: #include <stdint.h>
prog: #include <stdio.h>
prog: #include <string.h>
prog: 
prog: int main(int argc, char **argv)
prog: {
prog: 	int big = 0;
prog: 	if (argc > 2 || (argc == 2 && strcmp(argv[1], "be") != 0 && strcmp(argv[1], "le") != 0)) {
prog: 		fprintf(stderr, "usage: u32dump [be]\n");
prog: 		return 2;
prog: 	}
prog: 	if (argc == 2)
prog: 		big = argv[1][0] == 'b';
prog: 
prog: 	unsigned char b[4];
prog: 	size_t n;
prog: 	while ((n = fread(b, 1, sizeof b, stdin)) == sizeof b) {
prog: 		uint32_t v;
prog: 		if (big)
prog: 			v = (uint32_t)b[0] << 24 | (uint32_t)b[1] << 16 | (uint32_t)b[2] << 8 | b[3];
prog: 		else
prog: 			v = (uint32_t)b[3] << 24 | (uint32_t)b[2] << 16 | (uint32_t)b[1] << 8 | b[0];
prog: 		printf("%lu\n", (unsigned long)v);
prog: 	}
prog: 	if (n != 0) {
prog: 		fprintf(stderr, "u32dump: %zu stray bytes at the end\n", n);
prog: 		return 1;
prog: 	}
prog: 	return 0;
prog: }
case: printf '\001\000\000\000\377\377\377\377' | u32dump
case: printf '\001\000\000\000\377\377\377\377' | u32dump be
case: printf '\000\000\001\000\200\000\000\000' | u32dump
case: printf '\001\002\003' | u32dump 2>&1; echo $?
case: printf '' | u32dump; u32dump x 2>&1; echo $?
hint: fread four bytes at a time into an unsigned char array and assemble with shifts; widen to uint32_t before shifting
